# Cursor Linux app-auth verification

Captured: 2026-09-10
Reference: https://github.com/steipete/CodexBar/pull/1908 (merged 2026-07-05)
Upstream PR: `feat(linux): support Cursor provider via app auth token`; final head `bdc9ae94a68dd35df7f2302ea5f65b70c93fc9d6`; merge `7807679535394caa78b75526caf433057377648f`
Contributor: @DonnieFi (PR author; credited in CHANGELOG.md)
Review baseline: 45cda6084d6415795053624b80ca3f8c05026580 (v0.57.0)
Reviewed source commit: ca649a5850f14d39cc90b262b7b2749ea5836035 (rebased onto upstream `main` at 7fdc176)
Host: Linux x86_64; Swift 6.3.3; release CLI

Summary
- Restore Linux Cursor usage through the signed-in app token, including Grok Bot, while preserving macOS browser flows and explicit web/manual semantics.
- Screenshot proof: [cursor_fix.png](../../cursor_fix.png) shows the Cursor, Third Party, and Grok Bot usage cards.

PR requirements checked
- Summary, maintainer improvements, behavior, proof, exact reviewed head, and contributor thanks are represented here or in the linked source/docs changes.
- The auth-provider merge-risk label used on upstream PR #1908 is called out as a review consideration; GitHub requires repository-admin rights to apply custom labels, so this fork PR remains unlabeled.

Team review outcome
- Standards review: absolute-only `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` handling is covered, including a literal `~/custom` regression case.
- Spec review: cached-session precedence, explicit-web isolation, app-token expiry, Grok Bot best-effort behavior, and read-only DB access are covered.
- PR audit: @DonnieFi attribution and the merged upstream PR head/merge commit are recorded above; no unverified co-author identity was added.

Scope
- Linux Cursor automatic/CLI auth reads the signed-in Cursor app state DB read-only.
- Manual cookies take precedence, followed by cached/stored sessions, then the app token.
- Explicit web mode requires a manual cookie and never reads app credentials.
- Cursor usage and the optional Grok Bot allowance are fetched independently.

